            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks, I can reproduce.\n\nAs `vii` doesn't work at line 1 column 1, but *does* work when on line 3, I do think this is a case when tree-sitter textobject just doesn't know about the language that human user wants it to.\n\nHere is the [relevant logic](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/be81509b7160aa02264a59789164a2e1c4a6750a/lua/mini/ai.lua#L1578-L1601). In this case line 1 column 1 indeed finds a \"markdown\" language tree and tries to find query matches in it and its parent (which are none). It does miss checking `markdown_inline`.\n\nBased this, the reverse *does* work: `vid` when on line 3 column 1 (i.e. inside `markdown_inline` language tree) does match the code cell.\n\n---\n\nI am a bit fuzzy on tree-sitter details, but I think it is the result of (deliberately) choosing #2010 approach instead of #2009 approach of solving the \"there is a query match in the language tree that is not current\". Mostly because of concerns in [this comment](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/2006#issuecomment-3290841201):\n\n> At the moment I don't think I like the idea because it introduces issues about how the textobject should be searched. Like if there is a non-covering textobject with injected language and covering textobject for parent language, which one should be chosen with `cover_or_next` search method. I'd probably expect the former, but I can see how the latter can be expected.\n\nThe problem with making the use case from this issue work is the problems of which from several matches to prefer:\n- Covering not in current language or next/last in current language.\n- Last in current language or next in current language *but* farther than the previous one.\n\n@TheLeoP, could you please double check if all of this makes sense?",
