        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I'll keep thinking about this and I'll add a new comment if I have any other idea.\n\nSounds good! The extra caveat is that it should work for both 'mini.ai' and 'mini.surround'.\n\n---\n\nThinking now, maybe doing the \"search current lang first, then all its parents, and then all its children; stop immediately if there are query matches\" *could* work overall? My problem with `:for_each_tree()` is that the traversal is not guaranteed to ordered and it has matches from all languages. The controlled traversal order and early stop seem okay, with extra performance cost being the only downside I can think of right now. Does *this* make sense?\n\nBut in any case, I'd still prefer to wait a bit (couple of days maybe) to see if there is any good way to resolve this. But in any case adding a [note](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/be81509b7160aa02264a59789164a2e1c4a6750a/doc/mini-ai.txt#L770-L778) with the eventual solution could help with linking for such cases in the future.",
